# ── Generated data ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# src/data/tool-popularity.json is written by a bot, not by hand:
# .github/workflows/update-popularity.yml runs scripts/update-popularity.mjs on a
# daily cron, commits the result and pushes to main. So the remote copy is always
# fresher than anything local, and a local edit to it is guaranteed to collide the
# next time you pull.
#
# take-remote resolves that automatically by keeping the incoming version. That is
# always the right answer here: the file is derived from Umami, so nothing in it is
# authored - if the numbers are wrong the fix is in the SCRIPT, and the next cron
# run regenerates the data anyway.
#
# ONE-TIME SETUP, per clone (git will not run a custom driver without it):
#
#     git config merge.take-remote.name "keep the incoming copy of a generated file"
#     git config merge.take-remote.driver "cp -f %B %A"
#
# On Windows without a cp on PATH, use:
#
#     git config merge.take-remote.driver "copy /Y %B %A"
#
# Without that config git falls back to a normal merge and just asks you to resolve,
# which is the behaviour you have today - so this is safe to commit either way.
src/data/tool-popularity.json merge=take-remote

# ── Line endings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Normalise text in the repo, check out native. Keeps a Windows clone from turning
# every file into a whitespace diff for anyone on another platform.
* text=auto
